---
title: "Grid State"
---

This section covers saving and restoring the grid state, such as the filter model, selected rows, etc.

## Saving and Restoring State

The following example shows how the current grid state can be saved and restored.

- Try sorting / re-sizing / hiding a column / apply a filter / scroll the grid / change the current page
- Click Recreate Grid with Current State to destroy the grid and re-create using the saved state
- Note how the grid returns to the same state

{% gridExampleRunner title="Grid State" name="grid-state"  exampleHeight=630 /%}

### Saving State

The current grid state can be retrieved by listening to the `stateUpdated` event, which is fired with the latest state when it changes. The state is also available on the [Grid Pre-Destroyed Event](./grid-lifecycle/#grid-pre-destroyed), which can be used to get the state when the grid is destroyed.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-events/events.json" section="gridLifecycle" names=["stateUpdated", "gridPreDestroyed"] /%}

The state can also be retrieved on demand via the `gridApi.getState()` method.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="gridState" names=["getState"] /%}

{% note %}
It is the application's responsibility to persist the grid state to a suitable location.
{% /note %}

### Sparse State Object

The state object only records what differs from the grid's defaults. Within a section, only the affected columns are listed: `sortModel` names the sorted columns, `hiddenColIds` the hidden ones and `leftColIds` the pinned ones. A section with nothing to record is omitted entirely, so a grid with no sort applied has no `sort` property at all.

```js
// State saved from a grid where the user sorted by gold and pinned athlete.
// No filter, row group or hidden columns were set, so those sections are absent.
{
    version: '36.2.0',
    sort: { sortModel: [{ colId: 'gold', sort: 'desc' }] },
    columnPinning: { leftColIds: ['athlete'], rightColIds: [] },
    columnOrder: { orderedColIds: ['athlete', 'gold', 'silver', 'country'] },
    columnSizing: { columnSizingModel: [{ colId: 'athlete', width: 150 }] },
}
```

Because of this, an absent property means "none" rather than "unspecified". When a user clears every sort and saves, the saved state has no `sort` section, and restoring it must leave the grid unsorted. This matters when restoring a state that supplies only some of the column state properties, as covered in [Column State and ColDefs](#column-state-and-coldefs).

### Restore via Initial State

To restore a saved state to a new grid, pass the saved state to the grid option `initialState`. It is read once on creation, applying filters and groups before any data renders, so there's no flash of unfiltered or ungrouped data.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    initialState: {
        filter: {
            filterModel: {
                year: {
                    filterType: 'set',
                    values: ['2012'],
                }
            }
        },
        columnVisibility: {
            hiddenColIds: ['athlete'],
        },
        rowGroup: {
            groupColIds: ['athlete'],
        }
        // other state properties
    }
}
```

### Restore via API

To restore state on an existing grid use the `api.setState(state)` method. For example, to enable a user to save multiple configurations of the same grid, and be able to switch between them on demand.

```js
// Restore a saved state onto an existing grid
api.setState(savedState);
```

The example below stores three full grid states, each captured with `api.getState()` after a user configured the grid, and applies one whenever a button is clicked.

- **Default View** resets the grid to its column definitions.
- **Medals by Country** groups by Country, sums the medal columns, hides the Athlete, Sport and Year columns and sorts by Total.
- **Swimming Leaders** filters Sport to Swimming, pins Athlete and sorts by Gold, Silver than Bronze.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Switching Between Saved States" name="saved-views"  exampleHeight=500 /%}

{% note %}
`setState` should only be used to restore grid state. The grid does not support being used as a controlled component, so do not call this on every state update.
{% /note %}

## Excluding Properties

It is possible to exclude properties from the save and restore process, for example, the application may not wish to restore the `scroll` position. The following sections explain how this is achieved for [Initial State](#excluding-via-initial-state) and [`gridApi.setState()`](#excluding-via-api).

### Excluding via Initial State

To exclude a property, either delete the property or set it to `undefined` on the `initialState` object. A grid state section that is omitted, such as `scroll` or `filter`, is simply not applied and the grid starts with its normal defaults for that property.

```js
// Restore everything except the scroll position and the row selection.
initialState: {
    ...savedState,
    scroll: undefined,
    rowSelection: undefined,
}
```

### Column State and ColDefs

If some but not all of the [column state properties](#gridstate-column-properties) are included in initialState the default behaviour of a missing column state properties is to reset those values, overriding their corresponding column definition value. This is to be consistent with the [sparse nature](#sparse-state-object) of the grid state object.

So in the snippet below, as at least one column state property is provided, `columnOrder`, all the other column states are inferred as empty. This means to correctly restore a users state

```js
columnDefs: [
    { field: 'athlete' },
    { field: 'country', sort: 'asc' },
],

initialState: {
    // Only Column Order provided, other columns states will be reset
    columnOrder: {
        orderedColIds: ['gold', 'silver', 'athlete', 'sport', 'country', 'year'],
    },
}
```

When saving and restoring full states, the grid optimises the size of the state object by only including state that is different from the empty case: an unsorted column is not listed in `sortModel`, and a grid with no sort has no `sort` section at all. When this state is restored the grid translates a missing `sort` state as "no sort is applied". This must override any default sorts applied on the column definitions to be correct.

It is possible to override this behaviour so that a missing column state falls back to the column definition value by setting `partialColumnState : true` on the initialState object.

When `initialState` supplies at least one of the [column state properties](#gridstate-column-properties), all the other column state properties that are omitted are treated as "reset". In other words, the same as if an end user has cleared that columns state, i.e cleared all default sorts applied via column definitions. If the column definition includes defaults, such as `hide`, `pinned` or `aggFunc` these are overridden and cleared. To exclude a column state section and keep its defaults, set `partialColumnState: true`.

`getState()` only records what differs from the default: an unsorted column is not listed in `sortModel`, and a grid with no sort has no `sort` section at all. So when a user clears every sort and saves, the saved state omits `sort` entirely, and restoring it must clear any sort the column definitions supply. Absence means "none", not "unspecified". `partialColumnState` flips that meaning, which is only correct for a state you built by hand rather than one saved from the grid.

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="GridState" overrideSrc="grid-state/grid-state.json" names=["partialColumnState"] config={ "description": "" } /%}

In the example below the column definitions pin Country, hide Year and sum Gold and Silver into a grand total row. Each button recreates the grid with a different `initialState`.

- **No State (column defaults)** shows the grid as defined by the column definitions.
- **Column Order Only (defaults cleared)** supplies only `columnOrder`. Country is unpinned, Year appears and the totals are blank.
- **Column Order Only + partialColumnState (defaults kept)** supplies the same `columnOrder` with `partialColumnState: true`. The order is applied and the column definition defaults remain.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Partial Column State" name="partial-column-state"  exampleHeight=500 /%}

`partialColumnState` only affects the column state sections. It has no effect on the grid state sections, which `initialState` never resets, and it is ignored by `api.setState()`, which resets every omitted section unless it is listed in `propertiesToIgnore` as described [below](#excluding-via-api).

### Excluding via API

`api.setState()` takes a second parameter called `propertiesToIgnore`. This lists the top-level sections to leave as they are. For example, `api.setState(state, ['filter'])` restores everything except the filters.

Every section the provided state omits is reset. A state containing only `columnOrder` also clears the sort, filters, row groups, pinning and every other section.

```js
// Restore a saved state but keep whatever the user has filtered since.
api.setState(savedState, ['filter']);
```

```js
// Hand-crafted state containing only the column order. Every other property is reset to
// its default: the sort, filters, row groups, pinning and so on are all cleared.
api.setState({
    columnOrder: {
        orderedColIds: ['gold', 'silver', 'athlete'],
    },
});
```

The example below starts with Country pinned, Gold sorted and a summed grand total row. Sort, filter or group the grid, then compare the buttons.

- **Save State** and **Restore State** round trip the full state. Anything changed after saving is undone, including the filters.
- **Restore State, Keep Filter** passes `['filter']` as `propertiesToIgnore`, so the current filters survive the restore.
- **Set Column Order Only** sets a state containing just `columnOrder`. The sort, pinning and aggregation are reset along with the filters and groups.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Partial State with setState" name="set-state-partial"  exampleHeight=500 /%}

## Compatibility

### Column and Group Ids

Columns are identified in the state by their [Column ID](./column-updating-definitions/#matching-columns), and [Column Groups](./column-groups/) by their `groupId`. A column that provides neither `colId` nor `field` is given a positional ID. If the column definitions are reordered between saving and restoring, column state could be restored to the wrong column.

{%note %}
For best results ensure columns and groups have unique `field` or explicit `colId` and groups have an explicit `groupId`.
{%/note %}

### Grid State API vs Column State API

Grid state and [Column State](./column-state/) overlap: the `sort`, `rowGroup`, `aggregation`, `showValuesAs`, `pivot`, `columnPinning`, `columnVisibility`, `columnSizing` and `columnOrder` sections of grid state carry the same information as Column State, in a different shape. Which one to reach for depends on what you're doing:

- **Grid state** saves and restores the grid as a whole, columns included, typically round-tripped through storage between sessions. Every column-related section it supplies replaces that aspect for every column, so a section that lists only some columns resets the rest.
- **Column State** targets one aspect of the columns directly, in place, without touching anything else. Use `api.applyColumnState()` for a one-off change such as reordering or resizing a subset of columns while leaving sort, filters and the rest of the grid untouched. See [Applying Partial State](./column-state/#partial-state) for examples.

If code was already using Column State alongside other custom APIs to manage grid-level state by hand, `convertColumnState` and `convertColumnGroupState` convert that Column State into the equivalent grid state sections:

```
const state = {
    ...convertColumnState(columnState),
    ...convertColumnGroupState(columnGroupState)
};
```

### Versioned State

The grid state includes the version it was generated from. This enables grid states generated on older versions of the grid to be consumed by newer versions of the grid. If there is a change in format the grid will automatically apply the conversion to the old state.

### Serializable State

The grid state is designed to be serialisable, so any functions will be stripped out. This has implications for features such as Aggregation. If a custom aggregation function has been provided it must be [Registered as Custom Functions](./aggregation-custom-functions/#registering-custom-functions) via a string, as opposed to directly registering the function with the column. This enables the grid state to safely store the `aggFunc` string value against the column.

